# The shares of executable functions and of their lines that the verus
# check holds main to (verus/count --floor; verus/README.md, "Coverage"):
# the counter's `shipped` row, {SHIPPED_CRATES: every crate of the port
# but one whose Cargo.toml says `publish = false` (name those); or, where
# verus/count.toml's `ships` says otherwise, the crates it ships (a single
# unpublished crate that ships: "every function under src/ but test
# code")}, rounded down to a tenth of a percent.
#
# The four figure names are the ones the kit's counter reads.
# `functions` and `lines` are the verified share: Verus checks them.
# `checked-functions` and `checked-lines` add the trusted functions Kani
# discharges ({DISCHARGED_BY: the `discharged_by = "kani"` marker,
# verus/README.md, "Kani"; or, with the generated-ledger back end, the
# seams the ledger marks discharged, verus/SEAMS.md, "Status"}), which
# hold on the bounded inputs their records under verus/seams/ name.
#
# New executable code comes with its proofs (CONTRIBUTING.md), so a change
# keeps each share at or above these figures. The check passes a share
# above its figure and names the figure to record; when it is recorded is
# the port's choice, so keep one of these and delete the other:
# {RAISE_RULE: same PR: a change that raises a share raises its figure
# here, to the one the check names, in the same pull request. When two
# pull requests both raise it, the second to merge takes the figures its
# run on the new base names. | own PR: the figures rise in their own pull
# requests between merges, to the figures the check names, so that pull
# requests landing in parallel do not conflict here.}
#
# README.md's Verus badge gives `functions` and `lines` as written here
# (verus/count.toml's `[badge]`), and the check fails when it does not, so
# the change that moves either figure replaces the badge line with the one
# `verus-count --badge .` prints.
#
# A figure set below the base revision's fails unless a
# `lowered FIGURE P REASON` line here, new in that change, names that
# figure and value.
#
# A `lowered` line is the last resort: close the gap by verifying more
# code first. Its REASON names exactly what became trusted or outside, and
# the counts before and after (`N of M shipped functions verified, P%,
# against main's SHA at N' of M'`).
#
# kit: proved-port kit/v{KIT_VERSION}
functions 0.0
lines 0.0
checked-functions 0.0
checked-lines 0.0
